course: biology
lesson: 267
title: Horns, frills, plates and clubs: Triceratops, Stegosaurus, Ankylosaurus and the duck-bills
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Plant-eating dinosaurs could not run from every hunter, so many grew horns, frills, plates, spikes and clubs, and each one tells its own story.
explain: Triceratops had three horns and a huge bony frill, lived at the same time and place as T. rex, and one Triceratops bone has T. rex bite marks that healed, so it got away.
explain: Stegosaurus had two rows of tall thin plates for show and four tail spikes that were a real weapon: one bone of Allosaurus, a big meat-eater, has a hole that a spike fits into exactly.
explain: Ankylosaurus was a living tank, covered in bony plates with a heavy club on its tail that could break a predator's leg with one swing.
explain: Others had no armour at all: duck-billed dinosaurs ground plants with hundreds of packed teeth, honked through hollow crests and looked after their babies in busy nesting colonies.
fact: Triceratops had three horns and a big bony frill, weighed as much as an elephant or more, and lived at the same time and place as T. rex.
fact: The frill was probably for showing off and recognising its own kind, with some protection; it changed shape as the animal grew, like a deer's antlers.
fact: Stegosaurus plates stood in two rows and were probably for display, maybe warmth; they were too thin to be good armour, and its brain was only about the size of a lime.
fact: The idea that Stegosaurus had a second brain in its hip is a myth; the space there held nerves and perhaps energy-rich tissue, as in birds' hips today.
fact: The four tail spikes of Stegosaurus are called a thagomizer, a name scientists borrowed from a funny cartoon; an Allosaurus bone has a hole that fits a spike.
fact: Ankylosaurus was covered with bony plates in its skin and had a heavy club on its tail that could break a predator's leg.
fact: Borealopelta is an armoured dinosaur kept so well that its skin and armour look like a statue; traces of colour show it was reddish-brown on top and paler underneath.
fact: Duck-billed dinosaurs, or hadrosaurs, such as Edmontosaurus had hundreds of teeth packed together to grind plants.
fact: Parasaurolophus had a long hollow crest that probably worked like a trumpet, making deep honking sounds.
fact: Maiasaura, the «good mother lizard», nested in colonies, and nests with babies of different sizes show the young were cared for.
fact: Pachycephalosaurus had a bony dome up to 25 centimetres thick; scientists argue whether it head-butted like goats, and some domes have healed injuries, which supports the head-butting idea.
fact: Many dinosaurs were small, some the size of a chicken, and the smallest living dinosaur is the bee hummingbird.
exercise: How many horns did Triceratops have? | Three | One | Two | Seven
exercise: Did Triceratops and T. rex live at the same time? | Yes, in the same time and place | No, millions of years apart | No, Triceratops lived in the sea | Only in films
exercise: What was the frill of Triceratops probably mostly for? | Showing off and recognising its own kind | Gliding short distances from tree to tree | Scooping up fish from shallow rivers | Storing water for the long dry season
exercise: How did the frill change as Triceratops grew? | It changed shape, like a deer's antlers | It fell off once the animal was fully grown | It stayed exactly the same from egg to old age | It slowly grew into a second little head
exercise: What were the plates on Stegosaurus probably for? | Display and maybe warmth | Strong armour against every bite | Cutting down trees | Swimming like fins
exercise: Did Stegosaurus have a second brain in its hip? | No, the space there held nerves and maybe energy-rich tissue | Yes, it had two brains of the same size, one in the head, one in the hip | Yes, and its hip brain was even bigger than its head brain | Yes, and it used the hip brain to work its back legs
exercise: About how big was the brain of Stegosaurus? | About the size of a lime | About the size of a football | About the size of a car | About the size of a grain of rice
exercise: What is a thagomizer? | The spiky end of a Stegosaurus tail | The long horn on a Triceratops nose | The heavy club on an Ankylosaurus tail | The hollow crest of a duck-billed dinosaur
exercise: How do we know Stegosaurus spikes were real weapons? | An Allosaurus bone has a hole that a spike fits | A famous old cartoon showed the two of them fighting | Its spikes were made of hard metal, not bone | Stegosaurus had sharp spikes on its nose as well
exercise: What did Ankylosaurus have on its tail? | A heavy bony club | Four long spikes | A fin for swimming | A rattle like a snake
exercise: What is special about the fossil of Borealopelta? | Its skin and armour are kept so well it looks like a statue | It was found still alive, sleeping deep inside a cave | It is the biggest and heaviest dinosaur ever dug up anywhere | It is the only armoured dinosaur that had wings for flying
exercise: How did duck-billed dinosaurs eat plants? | They ground them with hundreds of packed teeth | They swallowed them whole without any teeth at all | They sucked up the juice through their bills like a straw | They only ate soft fruit that fell on the ground
exercise: What was the hollow crest of Parasaurolophus probably for? | Making deep honking sounds | Storing water for dry days | Breathing deep underwater | Stabbing enemies in fights
exercise: What does the name Maiasaura mean? | Good mother lizard | Tyrant lizard king | Thunder lizard | Egg thief
exercise: How thick could the bony dome of Pachycephalosaurus be? | Up to 25 centimetres | About 1 millimetre | About 2 metres | About as thick as paper
exercise: What is the smallest living dinosaur? | The bee hummingbird | The farmyard chicken | The house sparrow | The leopard gecko
test: «Stegosaurus had a brain in its bum to help it walk, read it in a book» Is that right? | No, that is an old myth; the space held nerves and maybe stored energy | Yes, it had two brains | Yes, but only babies had it | No, it had no brain at all
test: «Triceratops and T. rex never met, T. rex came way later» Is that right? | No, they lived at the same time and place | Yes, T. rex came millions of years later | Yes, Triceratops lived much later, with people | No, T. rex lived only in the sea
test: Count them: 2 Triceratops and 3 Stegosaurus stand in a museum. How many horns on the Triceratops and spikes on the Stegosaurus tails in all? | 6 horns and 12 spikes | 6 horns and 6 spikes | 3 horns and 12 spikes | 5 horns and 8 spikes
test: «Stego's back plates, that's armour innit, like a knight» What is closer to the truth? | They were too thin for good armour, probably for display | Yes, they were thick armour that stopped every single bite | They were made of metal, like a real knight's armour | They were folded-up wings it opened to fly away
test: What would happen if an Allosaurus grabbed an Ankylosaurus from the side? | It might get its leg broken by the tail club | It would easily bite through the armour | The Ankylosaurus would fly away | The Ankylosaurus would turn into a ball and roll away
test: A Parasaurolophus model has a hollow crest. A museum blows air through it. What do you expect? | A deep honk, like a trumpet | Nothing, the crest is solid | A high whistle like a bird | A bang like a drum
test: Scientists find nests with babies of different sizes still in them. What does that suggest? | The parents cared for the babies after hatching | The babies had been stolen from other dinosaurs' nests | The babies never grew at all after they hatched | The nests were really made by small furry mammals
test: «dinosaurs were all massive, every single one» What is right? | No, many were small, some the size of a chicken | Yes, even the very smallest was as big as a bus | Yes, small dinosaurs never existed at all, anywhere | No, they were all tiny, about the size of ants
test: Why do Pachycephalosaurus domes with healed injuries matter? | They support the idea that they head-butted | They prove that it could fly up into the trees | They show that it chewed and swallowed rocks | They show it had no proper skull at all
test: How could scientists tell the colour of Borealopelta? | From tiny traces of colour kept in its fossil skin | From a painting made by people at the time | By guessing from the colours in an old cartoon | From the colour of the rock it was found in
test: «the frill on a baby Triceratops looks well different to its mum's» Why? | Frills changed shape as the animal grew | The baby was a different kind of dinosaur | Mums broke their frills fighting | Babies had no bones yet
exam: Identify the function most probably served by the dorsal plates of Stegosaurus. | Display, and possibly the regulation of temperature | Impenetrable armour against all predators | Propulsion through water | The cutting of tree trunks
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the purported second brain of Stegosaurus. | It is a discredited notion; the cavity in the hip accommodated nerves and possibly stored tissue | It is confirmed that the animal possessed two brains of equal size | It is confirmed that the second brain exceeded the first in size | It is confirmed that the animal possessed no brain whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the principal defensive structure borne upon the tail of Ankylosaurus. | A heavy bony club | A set of four elongated spikes | A venomous sting | A broad swimming fin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dentition of the hadrosaurs? | They possessed hundreds of closely packed teeth adapted to the grinding of plants | They were entirely without teeth and swallowed vegetation whole without processing | They possessed a single pair of tusks employed in the uprooting of plants | They possessed teeth adapted solely to the tearing of flesh from carcasses
